  "answers": {
    "Q3": "Great question! Let’s walk through the geometry together.\n\nA ray can be written as  \n\n\\[\nP(t) = O + tD \\quad (t > 0)\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n- \\(O\\) is the ray origin,  \n- \\(D\\) is a normalized direction vector,  \n- \\(t\\) is the distance along the ray.\n\nA sphere is defined by its center \\(C\\) and radius \\(r\\). Any point \\(P\\) on the sphere satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|P - C|^2 = r^2 .\n\\]\n\n**What do you get if you substitute the ray equation into the sphere equation?**\n\nThink about expanding \\(|(O + tD) - C|^2\\). What kind of equation will you have in terms of \\(t\\)?\n\n*(Hint: it will be a quadratic \\(at^2 + bt + c = 0\\).)*\n\nOnce you have the quadratic, recall that the discriminant \\(b^2-4ac\\) tells you whether the ray:\n\n- misses the sphere (negative),\n- grazes it (zero),\n- intersects it (positive).\n\n**After you find the roots, which one gives the first intersection point you care about?**\n\nThink about the sign of \\(t\\) and which root is smaller.\n\nFeel free to write out the algebra; I’ll check your work and guide you further!"
